  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nMain focus of the forecast continues to be the approaching\nstrong cold front and associated convective potential. Right now\nthere is an upper low swirling over Texas and a diving \nshortwave trough over the central Plains that is expected to \npick up a cold front and help spin up/deepen a surface low over \nIL/IN/OH that will be deepening modestly as it passes north. \nSouthwesterly flow picks up later this morning at the surface \nand advects in air over central Texas that sits with dewpoints \nat about 58-60 degrees. The geometry of the upper level pattern \nlooks to prevent any of the richer moisture from the western \nGulf and deep south Texas (65-70 dewpoints) from getting pulled\ninto the stream moving it into this area.\n\nThe end result is dewpoints building to about 55-59 in the \nafternoon ahead of the front. The RRFS and HRRR produce a brief\nwindow of about 900 J/kg of ML-ECAPE (Mixed layer entrainment\nfactored CAPE). 850 winds are southwesterly at 25 to 30 kts \nwith about 90 kts at 250 mb. This gives us some moderate speed \nshear with a fairly straight hodograph. 3-6km lapse rates are \nfairly steep at 6.8-6.9 C/km with FZLs about 9000-10000 ft. As \nhas been modeled for several days column RH is highest at the \nvery top of the boundary layer and I think its possible we get \nvery shallow showers that are pseudo-elevated even as precip\ngets going. REFS members are very slow to develop deep \nconvection, with it holding off until leaving the forecast area \nto the east. I think its also possible the surface cold front \ntracks slightly faster than modeled given the shallow dense cold\nairmass, although modeling is getting a lot better at timing \nthese than in years past. If a perfect situation comes together \nand you get a surface based cell 900-1000 J/kg is sufficient for\nsome wind and possibly small hail threat given the shear and \nthermodynamic profiles. I think the most likely outcome still \n(85% chance) is for deep convection to fail to initiate in the \nCWA. If not we will have a few storms to watch by 3-5 pm in the \neasternmost part of the CWA.\n\nStill looks much colder behind the front. Highs look to range\n47-52 or so through Monday and trending colder with overnight lows\nbelow freezing through next Tuesday. A shortwave passes\nbasically directly overhead early next week and generate precip\nand a low to our south. This would keep the area in a cool\nairmass with mostly dry conditions into the middle of next week.",
